// boot.hex -- reset-vector / execution-pointer disambiguation test, v2.
//
// The ROM (sim/rtl/axi_sim_mem.v) default-fills as a flat NOP (0x90) sled
// across the whole 128KiB window. This file overrides only the bytes
// near physical 0xFFC00, which fetch-address tracing showed the core
// hitting during an early "purge" boot sequence, AND which dbg_pc_out
// (cpu's real committed PC) showed as the genuine execution start point
// -- contradicting the useq.v addr_reg reset-vector edit on this branch,
// which only affects the fetch-address (iaddr) register, not pc_out.
//
// v1 of this trap used HLT (0xF4) and it did not stop execution --
// either HLT isn't implemented, or there's a fetch/decode timing offset
// that made a single-byte trap ambiguous. v2 replaces it with a genuine
// long jump instead: a near JMP with a 32-bit relative displacement
// (0x66 operand-size prefix + 0xE9), landing at physical 0xF00000 --
// deliberately far outside every mapped region (RAM [0,0xA0000), ROM
// [0xE0000,0x100000)) so there is no other plausible source for fetch/PC
// activity at that exact address. Chosen over a short relative self-loop
// specifically because a genuine long jump should force a real pipeline
// redirect/refetch, instead of something a tight loop might handle
// ambiguously (as apparently happened with the +2-per-step dbg_pc_out
// stepping we saw walking straight through the HLT byte).
//
//   physical 0xFFBFE-0xFFBFF (ROM offset 0x1FBFE): EB 06         JMP +6
//   physical 0xFFC00-0xFFC05 (ROM offset 0x1FC00): 66 E9 FA 03 E0 00
//                                                   JMP near 0x00F00000
//
// JMP +6 skips exactly the 6-byte trap instruction for anything that
// merely walks the NOP sled past this point sequentially -- it will NOT
// jump to 0xF00000 unless something lands directly on 0xFFC00.
//
// This core evidently supports 32-bit operand-size prefixed instructions
// (cr0/cr3/paging signals are present elsewhere in the design, i.e. this
// is a real protected-mode-capable core), which is why a 0x66-prefixed
// rel32 near jump was chosen over a 16-bit far jump (ptr16:16, opcode
// 0xEA) -- the latter's max reachable physical address in real mode is
// only ~0x10FFEF, nowhere near 0xF00000.
//
// If dbg_pc_out (or the m00_AXI AR fetch address) is ever observed at
// exactly 0x00F00000, that's unambiguous confirmation real execution
// reached and jumped from 0xFFC00.
//
// Boundary guard, added to allow much longer runs without the fetch/PC
// walk running off the mapped ROM entirely: axi_sim_mem.v does not
// implement real 8086-style 20-bit address wraparound (0xFFFFF+1 does
// NOT wrap to 0x00000 here -- see sim/README.md's "Known limitations"),
// so a plain NOP sled would just walk forever into unmapped space past
// 0xFFFFF. Instead, a relative jump near the very end of the ROM bounces
// execution back into the mapped region before it can run off the edge:
//
//   physical 0xFFFFC-0xFFFFE (ROM offset 0x1FFFC): E9 7E FF
//                                                   JMP near $-127 (-> 0xFFF7D)
//
// This is a testbench-side control measure (in the boot image, not the
// RTL or the memory model) -- it does not change core_rtl behavior, it
// just keeps a long run confined to addresses this testbench actually
// modeled a real hardware response for.
@1fbfe
EB 06
@1fc00
66 E9 FA 03 E0 00
@1fffc
E9 7E FF
